      }
    },
    {
      "page_id": "PAGE002",
      "page_number": 2,
      "page_type": "concept",
      "pedagogical_role": "explanation",
      "learning_objective_ids": [
        "OBJ001"
      ],
      "title": "From A to The",
      "content": [
        {
          "block_id": "BLOCK003",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The first time we mention something, we use a. After that, the listener knows which one, so we use the."
        },
        {
          "block_id": "BLOCK004",
          "type": "example",
          "examples": [
            {
              "sentence": "I bought a notebook and a pen. The notebook cost more.",
              "highlight": [
                "a notebook",
                "The notebook"
              ],
              "explanation": "A notebook introduces it; the notebook refers back to the same one."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "key_takeaway": "First a, then the.",
      "page_audit": {
        "primary_objective_count": 1,
        "estimated_visible_word_count": 40,
        "density_status": "passed",
        "standalone_understanding": "passed"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_id": "PAGE003",
      "page_number": 3,
      "page_type": "concept",
      "pedagogical_role": "explanation",
      "learning_objective_ids": [
        "OBJ001"
      ],
      "title": "Things We Both Know",
      "content": [
        {
          "block_id": "BLOCK005",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use the for something both speakers know about, such as things in the same room or home."
        },
        {
          "block_id": "BLOCK006",
          "type": "example",
          "examples": [
            {
              "sentence": "Switch off the fan before you leave.",
              "highlight": [
                "the fan"
              ],
              "explanation": "Both people know which fan: the one in their room."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "key_takeaway": "If we both know which one, use the.",
      "page_audit": {
        "primary_objective_count": 1,
        "estimated_visible_word_count": 30,
        "density_status": "passed",
        "standalone_understanding": "passed"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_id": "PAGE004",
      "page_number": 4,
      "page_type": "concept",
      "pedagogical_role": "explanation",
      "learning_objective_ids": [
        "OBJ002"
      ],
      "title": "Only One in the World",
      "content": [
        {
          "block_id": "BLOCK007",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use the for things there is only one of: the sun, the moon, the sky, the internet."
        },
        {
          "block_id": "BLOCK008",
          "type": "example",
          "examples": [
            {
              "sentence": "The moon looked orange tonight.",
              "highlight": [
                "The moon"
              ],
              "explanation": "There is only one moon, so we say the moon."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "key_takeaway": "Only one exists? Use the.",
      "page_audit": {
        "primary_objective_count": 1,
        "estimated_visible_word_count": 30,
        "density_status": "passed",
        "standalone_understanding": "passed"
      }
    },
    {
      "page_id": "PAGE005",
      "page_number": 5,
      "page_type": "concept",
      "pedagogical_role": "explanation",
      "learning_objective_ids": [
        "OBJ002"
      ],
      "title": "A Phrase That Picks One Out",
      "content": [
        {
          "block_id": "BLOCK009",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Words after the noun can pick out one particular thing. Then we use the."
        },
        {
          "block_id": "BLOCK010",
          "type": "example",
          "examples": [
            {
              "sentence": "The bag on the chair belongs to my cousin.",
              "highlight": [
                "The bag on the chair"
              ],
              "explanation": "'On the chair' tells us exactly which bag."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "key_takeaway": "If a phrase shows which one, use the.",
